OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) Model
It has 7 layers:
  -Application
  -Presentation
  -Session
  -Transport
  -Network
  -Data Link
  -Physical

Application Layer: Layer with which the user directly interacts for doing the desirable tasks or sending data (e.g., HTTP, FTP, SMTP).
Presentation Layer: Handles data translation, formatting/syntax, data compression, and encryption/decryption.
Session Layer: Establishes, manages, and terminates connections; handles authentication, authorization, and session checkpoints.
Transport Layer: Ensures process-to-process (application-to-application) delivery via port numbers, handles segmentation, flow control, 
and error recovery.
Network Layer: Handles logical addressing (IP addresses), packet routing from source to destination across different networks, 
and optimal path selection.
Data Link Layer: Converts packets into frames and manages node-to-node physical delivery using MAC addresses on the same local network.
Physical Layer: Transmits raw bit streams (0s and 1s) over physical media through hardware, cables, connectors, and electrical signals.
